$DIR/main.p:33:7: Count returns a closure that captures its parameters, which is built in its caller's frame; it can only be called directly, not used as a func value
    This function's body returns a function literal that captures the function's
    parameters, such as an iterator constructor (func Count(n int) iter.Seq[int]
    { return func(yield func(int) bool) { ... } }). A closure may not outlive
    the frame it captures from, so Plutonium builds such a closure in the
    caller's frame: each call is compiled where it is made, with the parameters
    as variables of the caller. That needs a caller, so the function can only be
    called directly. Taking it as a func value or a method value, or calling it
    through an interface, has no caller's frame to build the closure in.
    Call it directly, or wrap the call in a function that returns something
    other than the closure.
    In Go the captured parameters move to the heap and the closure can be
    returned. Plutonium never moves a variable to the heap, and building the
    closure in the caller's frame costs no allocation.
    Call it where the iterator is used:
        for i := range Count(3) {
            ...
        }
    (see "Closures do not outlive their frame" in the Plutonium spec, section
    4.5)
    (run "pluto explain E2543" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:17:3: defer in the body of a range-over-func loop must be followed by a return, or a break or goto out of the loop (otherwise it may run once per iteration, which would need unbounded stack)
    The body of a loop over a function (for x := range seq) runs once for each
    value the iterator yields, so a defer statement in it is a defer inside a
    loop. Its deferred call belongs to the function the loop is in and runs when
    that function returns, as in Go. Plutonium gives each defer statement one
    slot in that function's frame, so the defer may run at most once per call:
    after it, every path must leave the loop, by return, by panic, or by break,
    continue or goto to a statement outside the loop.
    If the deferred call should run at the end of each iteration, move the body
    into a function of its own.
    Go keeps the deferred calls of such a loop in a list on the heap. Plutonium
    never allocates behind the program's back, and kernel stacks are small and
    fixed.
    Leave the loop after the defer, or give the body a function of its own:
        for item := range items.All() {
            if item.ID == id {
                item.mu.Lock()
                defer item.mu.Unlock()
                return item.Value
            }
        }
    (see "Range over functions" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2533" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:24:10: a function literal deferred in a range-over-func loop body cannot capture x, a variable of the loop body: the deferred call runs after the body has returned
    A function literal deferred in the body of a loop over a function runs when
    the enclosing function returns, after the loop body has returned. The body's
    own variables, including the loop variables, live in the body's frame, which
    is gone by then, so the literal cannot capture them.
    Pass the values the deferred call needs as arguments: arguments are
    evaluated when the defer statement runs.
    Go moves such variables to the heap. Plutonium never moves a variable to the
    heap; a closure is valid only while the frame it captures from is.
    Pass the loop variable as an argument:
        for x := range seq {
            if done(x) {
                defer func(x int) { log(x) }(x)
                return
            }
        }
    (see "Range over functions" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2534" for a longer explanation)
